syria's refugees a lost generation  this week in the interview we speak to  christolina giorgieva european  commissioner for humanitarian aid and  crisis response  commissioner  it's been described as the worst  humanitarian crisis of the 21st century  so far the crisis in and around syria  now i know that you know the region very  well and i know that you're in almost  daily contact with humanitarian partners  in the region what can you tell me about  the situation on the ground  it is terrible it is the worst crisis  that we have lived through in the  last decades and unfortunately it would  get even worse  we have  tremendous suffering of syrians in the  country  the world sees more the faces of the  refugees the 2 million people who fled  but 5 million have been displaced  internally  and when i think of what this crisis is  i don't see a face of a soldier or a  rebel it is the face of a child  half of the victims of of the syria  crisis  are children  i have talked to many of them  and the stories they they share  are stories that no child should ever  have to face  a six-year boy gave me a present a  drawing  of his memories from home from syria and  it was a drawing  of  a tank  blood  and the dead body of his young cousin  abdullah  in syria  for a first time in the last decades  civilians are targeted and killed  doctors are shot at and the world is  turning a blind eye that's one thing i  wanted to talk about because you're  talking here about the suffering and  hardship of the syrian people the  displaced people and the refugees but  there are also people in syria aid  workers relief workers who are trying to  get help to them tell me about how their  situation has become more difficult and  more dangerous  even the most experienced humanitarian  workers tell me  we have never seen anything so bad  they are shot at  a convoy  humanitarian goods  leaves damascus  and travels for 16 days  under sniper fire they get stopped  multiple times  arrested released  and that sometimes things are taken out  of the of the of the convoy  we lost 31 humanitarian workers in this  crisis actually a german humanitarian  worker just  saved his life running out of a  captivity  and that is where  in my hearts of heart  i am stunned that the united nations  security council has not yet  voted on a resolution to condemn killing  of civilians and the people who are  there to help well tell me the word used  there is stunned i think that's a very  interesting when you talk about that  you've mentioned yourself in the past  the paralysis as you've called it of the  united nations security council you've  dismissed the council and i'm quoting  you here as an institution of the past  completely anachronistic in the 21st  century those are strong words  the united nations security council  is there to guarantee peace in the world  well at any one point of time  there are 20 to 30 countries in conflict  slipping into a conflict coming out of a  conflict  in the case of syria this is the first  time  when the security council has not been  able  to unite around two very simple things  don't kill civilians don't kill the  people who are there to help it has  never happened before  and when  how how angry does that make you  personally because you know the  situation in syria and you're describing  blocking tactics by the russians  primarily by the chinese as well how  angry does that make you  it makes me mostly very sad for the  people who suffer it's not about me it  is about  those who need help and those who ask  day in day out a very simple question  how come the world is turning a blind  eye  on what is happening uh to us how can we  actually take  what you're saying is you're saying the  world is turning a blind eye to this  crisis does that i mean you the eu is  the eu turning a blind eye to the crisis  the humanitarian community including us  in the european union from day one we  have been leaning forward to help people  as much as possible we actually we  european leaning forward is an  interesting formulation we have  mobilized  extraordinary amounts of support for the  people in syria and for their neighbors  we speak today and it is 1.5  billion euros  for medicines and food and shelter  to victims of the crisis in syria  for safe space for children it sounds  like a lot of money is it enough money  because i mean you know these countries  especially the four immediate neighbors  of syria they're being absolutely pushed  to the limit as you know  in terms of uh humanitarian contribution  this is very significant look  every year the world spends between  seven and 12 billion euros in  humanitarian aid for some three to four  hundred million people who need help so  this is very significant but the needs  are growing and i can say we cannot  catch up  we have negotiated that 14 international  humanitarian organizations can operate  inside syria health a german  organization is one of them it would  have not happened without our active  participation but we also were the first  to say look at the neighboring countries  not only we have to help the refugees  we have to help the local communities  that are hosting them our latest  contribution from the commission  400 million euros 250 humanitarian aid  150  million for  jordanian and lebanese local communities  so we don't see  heated  uh  confrontation between refugees and local  people you've said uh in the in the very  recent past you've said because we're  talking here about money and obviously a  significant contribution is being made  here but you have said  money is not enough and many european  countries are now taking in refugees  from syria but germany for example is  taking and has begun to take in 5 000  refugees  now rights activists and opposition  figures have already said  it's a drop in the ocean  they're right aren't they they're right  well yes and no because for the moment  the syrian people what do they want they  want an end to this madness  so they can go home  i talk to  hundreds and hundreds of refugees  they actually  are not so much looking to come to  europe some young people want to come to  study in europe but majority they are  culturally more comfortable where they  are so helping jordan lebanon turkey  iraq  this is really important  those who want to come we have already  accepted  close to 50 000  people and i would say we will have to  do more we have to keep our hearts open  our wallets open and our borders open  because  what we europeans bring  is this  notion that solidarity in the world is  the only way to face the future of more  disasters and more more when you talk  about the future there it's interesting  because really we we've so far only  really talked about the here and now of  the syria crisis what about all the  traumatized children what about the  cities that have been ravaged by the  conflict who's going to help those  children in the future who is going to  rebuild the cities  thank you for bringing the question of  children because  what we are producing with this war is  not just suffering and loss of life but  a lost generation  and  focusing today  on how we can alleviate this loss  for the sake of children because this is  morally right to do but also for our own  sake  uh and what we can do is and and this is  where the european union i'm sure would  play a very decisive role is to bring  the world together  on  raising money but also designing  programs for the lost generation even if  the crisis ends tomorrow  there is peace  the wounds  it would take years and years and years  to heal  and just tell me this i know you you're  a very busy commissioner and i know that  you've been on the ground in crisis  situations in countries like haiti and  pakistan in chile i think in somalia  it's fair to say those experiences that  you've gathered elsewhere how have they  helped you to lead your team  in syria  well what i learned is  read the early signs of crisis  and  i will go back to this expression that  you picked up lean forward  act decisively  target the most vulnerable people  and always  be agile for changing circumstances  deploy your very best people in the most  difficult conditions and i can tell you  maybe the most important thing i learned  in this job is pray for the best  prepare for the worst  that's interesting because that's  exactly the point i was going to come to  as my final question when when you  because you said this is not about you  as a person but i'd like to know about  you as a person when you go to these  places that have been that have been  very hard hit by conflicts or natural  catastrophes natural disasters  how close do you sometimes perhaps come  to losing hope  you know i cannot afford that  because people there face much more  difficult time  and i bring them i bring them help but i  also must  bring them hope i i have to i have to  have it in my heart  there is one picture that i would carry  with me as long as i live  i was in my  home country playing with my  granddaughter she was 18 months very  healthy  young baby  from there i went to chat so from  bulgaria to chad from bulgaria to chat  and i walked in this room  full of children 40 at least  so severely malnourished  that that not only they cannot play and  laugh  they can't even cry and the  deafening silence in this room i would  never forget  and i and i had to smile to them because  that was  what i can bring in this room  hope  thank you for that and thank you very  much for talking to us today  commissioner
 

